  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ResCoordDisc 5
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "At a meeting between lab researchers and the clinical team, you sit
    in to help guide the discussion. The researchers discuss new ideas for cancer
    treatments they\u2019re exploring and share their early findings from lab work,
    while the clinical staff explain what information will be needed to plan a future
    trial. Meanwhile, you take notes, ask clarifying questions, and explain how certain
    details \u2014 like how patient samples are stored \u2014 will matter later if
    early research moves toward trials.\n\nAs the research coordinator, what is your
    role in this situation?"
  explanation: The research coordinator helps support communication and planning
    between research and clinical teams so that early discoveries can transition
    smoothly into future clinical steps. Research coordinators do not design lab
    tests, nor do they write trial protocols alone; they also do not make patient
    selection decisions at this stage. Instead, they make sure the right information
    is shared and documented so both teams are prepared when the research is ready
    to move forward.
  correctAnswer: 'A: Acting as a bridge between research and clinical teams to support
    communication and plan for future trials'
  incorrectAnswer1: 'B: Deciding which safety tests that the research team should
    run on the new treatment in the lab'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'C: Writing the scientific protocol, or study plan, that will
    be used in future clinical trials'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'D: Making early decisions about which patients will be recruited
    to join the clinical trial'
  questionRole: Research Coordinator
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
